[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWMC-182
TITLE: HVPS Interface Board Communication
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware communicates with the external HVPS interface board via one of the microcontroller's serial UART interfaces. The firmware both receives and transmits data on this UART port. Due to the nature of UART having independent transmit and receive lines, the transmit and receive functionality operate independently. As the UART baud rate is relatively slow compared to the processing speed of the microcontroller, both sending and receiving data is performed with nonblocking interrupt callback functions.

Transmitted data is sent as a buffer of bytes, representing a singular data packet to the PC, with the interrupt callback occurring once all data bytes have been transmitted. The firmware sets a flag when transmission is initiated, and clears the flag after transmission is finished, to not create transmission conflicts on the transmit line.

Reception is performed one byte at a time, with the interrupt callback occurring once a data byte is available. The parsing of the received byte is done to determine whether a complete data packet has been received from the PC, as according to the communication protocol. Once a completed data packet has been received, the firmware sets a flag for further data processing outside of the parsing inside of the interrupt callback.

As transmission on UART is asynchronous both in terms of clock as well as word alignment (within a packet), a 'sync value' is utilized at the start of each packet to help synchronize communication in case of any lost or missing bytes.

When processing an HVPS status message that passes checksum validation, the firmware
toggles on-board LED4 once before copying and reporting the received status. The status
processing path does not toggle LED4 again, so repeated valid messages produce a visible
communication-activity blink.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-68
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWMC-183
TITLE: PC Ethernet Communication
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware communicates with the PC through an Ethernet interface using an RMII
transceiver and the LWIP UDP stack. Application traffic is separated into:

- Commands and their responses
- Unsolicited 100 Hz system-telemetry packets
- Unsolicited 1 Hz device-information packets
- Unsolicited fault-entry and fault-clear notifications

The PC can also request telemetry, device information, and an indexed fault entry. UDP is
used because timely delivery is more important than retransmitting stale real-time data.
Application payloads use the PC-Firmware Communication Protocol framing and CRC described
in the appendix.

During HVPS-interface initialization, the firmware queues command ``0x7F`` to request that
board's release version. The UART receive path recognizes the 13-word ``VRSN`` frame,
validates its additive checksum, and caches the returned 32-byte version field and
operating-mode identifier.

The 19-word PC device-information payload contains the main-control 32-byte release version,
application CRC, and operating mode, followed by the cached HVPS-interface 32-byte release
version and operating mode. Until a valid HVPS version response is received, the cached
version is ``unavailable`` and its operating-mode identifier is invalid.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-84
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-83
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-306
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWMC-184
TITLE: Peltier Cooler Communication
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware communicates with the external Peltier controller via one of the microcontroller's serial UART interfaces. The firmware both receives and transmits data on this UART port. Due to the nature of UART having independent transmit and receive lines, the transmit and receive functionality operate independently. As the UART baud rate is relatively slow compared to the processing speed of the microcontroller, both sending and receiving data is performed with nonblocking interrupt callback functions.

Transmitted data is sent as a buffer of bytes, representing a singular data packet to the PC, with the interrupt callback occurring once all data bytes have been transmitted. The firmware sets a flag when transmission is initiated, and clears the flag after transmission is finished, to not create transmission conflicts on the transmit line.

Reception is performed one byte at a time, with the interrupt callback occurring once a data byte is available. The parsing of the received byte is done to determine whether a complete data packet has been received from the PC, as according to the communication protocol. Once a completed data packet has been received, the firmware sets a flag for further data processing outside of the parsing inside of the interrupt callback.

The communication protocol is based on the pre-defined protocol defined by the off-the-shelf controller TC-48-20-OEM.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-64
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWMC-185
TITLE: Fault Handling
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware maintains a latched table of at most four active fault records. A record
contains the fault category, CRC-32 hash of the format string, clear epoch, entry index,
active count, captured system state, captured runtime, format-argument count, a
NUL-terminated 128-byte printable ASCII format string, and up to five 32-bit arguments.
Format specifiers identify unsigned integer, hexadecimal, floating-point, and literal
percent values so that the CNC can render a human-readable message.

Repeated reports with the same category and format are deduplicated while latched. A new
distinct record is published without waiting for a request. The PC retrieves a coherent
snapshot by requesting entries by index and checking the clear epoch and active count.
An out-of-range index returns an empty entry with the current snapshot metadata.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-6
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWMC-186
TITLE: PC Ethernet Data
STATEMENT: >>>
Each PC Ethernet communication packet contains a synchronization header, packet type,
packet counter, payload length, payload, and CRC footer. PC commands receive corresponding
responses. System telemetry, device information, new fault entries, and fault-clear
updates can also be transmitted without a preceding request. Packet types and payload
schemas are documented in the appendix section PC-Firmware Communication Protocol.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-46
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-84
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWMC-187
TITLE: State Machine
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware uses a state variable and queued events to execute treatment-plan and
emission state transitions. Events originate from PC commands, HVPS stabilization, timers,
and detected faults. Event identifiers outside the valid enumeration are rejected.

Valid events enter a circular FIFO with separate read and write indices and one reserved
slot. The producer and consumer each perform data and index updates inside a critical
section. The producer stores an event before publishing the new write index. The consumer
copies the next event to a local value before advancing the read index, so a concurrently
queued event cannot be partially published, overwritten, or dispatched twice. When the
next write index would equal the read index, the producer leaves the queue unchanged and
latches a ``FAULT_MEMORY`` record with the message ``State machine event queue overflow``.

The processing loop drains accepted events in FIFO order and dispatches each local event
through the function-table entry selected by the current state. The table defaults every
entry to the crash handler before known states are assigned. A state index outside the
table bounds is also routed directly to the crash handler. That handler disables the
high-voltage and emission interlocks, commands HVPS and coil outputs to zero, stops the
coolant pump and external indicators, and sets the system-crash state.
<<<
