[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWMC-296
TITLE: HVPS Monitoring: Faults
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware will continuously check at periodic intervals, various measured and reported HVPS feedback signals to monitor for faults. If any issues are detected, a fault condition may emerge. The following are monitored conditions for fault reporting:

- kV present when no kV should be present
- kV out of tolerance when kV should be present
- mA present when no mA should be present
- mA out of tolerance when mA should be present
- Filament overcurrent
- Occurrence of timeouts when waiting for HVPS values to stabilize
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-12
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-68
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWMC-297
TITLE: HVPS Monitoring: Stabilization
STATEMENT: >>>
The firmware will continuously check at periodic intervals, various measured and reported HVPS feedback signals to determine if the HVPS is stable at certain states. This determination of stability allows the progression of the state machine in the following states under the following conditions:

- Completion of the conditioning state by having the filament current reach the target filament current
- Completion of the warmup state by having the filament current reach the target filament current
- Completion of the setup state by having the high voltage reach the target kV
- Completion of the launching state by having the high voltage reach the target filament current
- Completion of the discharge state by having low high voltage and filament current
- Completion of the termination state by having low high voltage
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-21
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-71
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWMC-298
TITLE: Fault Clearing
STATEMENT: >>>
The main control board has hardware which latches fault conditions and disables interlocks
so that emission is safely terminated and cannot be resumed until action is taken by the
firmware. After the PC directive identifier and confirmation match for a clear-fault
request, the directive handler queues both the main-control clear-fault state-machine event
and an HVPS clear-fault command with zero-valued parameters. This directive has no state
gate. The handler leaves the PC directive response as successful without waiting for an
HVPS response; a confirmation mismatch returns an invalid response and queues neither
action.

The state-machine event pulses the hardware clear output, increments the software clear
epoch, empties the latched fault-record table, clears the software category flags, and
publishes an empty fault update. The firmware cannot override an active hardware fault; any
remaining condition re-latches the interlocks and can create a new software fault record.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-78
  ROLE: Refines
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-79
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWMC-299
TITLE: External Watchdog
STATEMENT: >>>
The main control board contains an external watchdog IC which can terminate emission. The firmware must send a periodically toggle digital output signal and reset the watchdog IC at least once per second, or the watchdog IC will terminate the emission.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-10
  ROLE: Refines

[DESIGN_ITEM]
UID: SW-H1FWMC-300
TITLE: Emission Magnetometer Fault Monitoring
STATEMENT: >>>
Setting the coils resets a three-sample magnetometer collector. Valid head-interface
packets fill the collector, and emission entry averages each of the six axes. If three
post-coil samples are unavailable, ``FAULT_MAGNETOMETER`` is reported and emission remains
disabled.

During emission, every valid head-interface packet is evaluated against the stored
baseline. Each axis of both magnetometers is monitored independently. The permitted
deviation is ten percent of the baseline magnitude, but is never less than 1.0 uT to avoid
false faults when a baseline is close to zero.

The firmware tracks consecutive out-of-range readings separately for every axis. An
in-range reading resets the count for that axis without affecting the other axes. A
magnetometer fault is reported when the same axis remains outside its permitted range for
three consecutive packets. A non-finite reading is considered invalid and reports a fault
immediately without waiting for additional packets.

The fault report identifies the affected magnetometer and axis and, when applicable,
includes the measured value, baseline, and permitted deviation. Reporting the fault
invalidates the stored baseline and requests a state-machine transition to the fault state.
<<<
RELATIONS:
